// Glass Library (glass)
//
// Transmissive materials. Each one is a zero-argument function returning a
// PAIR: its material (the finish and the medium) and its colour, in that
// order.
//   lensMat, lensColor = glass.clear();
//   lens.material = lensMat;
//   lens.color = lensColor;
// A transmissive material takes its tint from the shape's colour, so glass
// needs both properties. They are returned together rather than named
// separately so a medium cannot be paired with the wrong tint. For a
// transmissive material the colour is the light that comes through after
// `absorptionDistance` of the medium (a white colour, or a distance of 0, is
// untinted), the way `name.color` always is the albedo of what the surface
// itself scatters. Materials are `material` records (see the material
// library); vary one with `material.withRoughness(lensMat, 0.3)` or replace
// the tint distance with `material.withTransmission(lensMat, 1, 12)`.
//
// The viewer refracts the view ray into a transmissive shape and marches it,
// so parts embedded in glass are seen through it, and a rough finish blurs
// what is seen. Reflections are the studio lights and the sky.
//
// Self-contained (no library dependencies). Doc convention follows
// builtin/base.gsl: `///` lines are public docs.

/// A fully transmissive material: roughness in [0, 1], ior >= 1 (1.5 is
/// glass), absorptionDistance in mm (the depth at which the shape's colour
/// is what comes through; 0 = no tint). Same row layout material.make
/// produces, spelled out so the library needs no imports.
make(rough, index, absorptionDistance) {
  return [[rough, 0, index, 0], [0, 0, 0, 0], [1, absorptionDistance, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0, 0]];
}

// ===========================================================================
// Glass
// ===========================================================================

/// Clear polished glass. Returns material and colour.
@material clear() {
  return make(0.02, 1.52, 0), [1, 1, 1];
}

/// Frosted glass: sand-blasted or acid-etched, blurs what is behind it.
/// Returns material and colour.
@material frosted() {
  return make(0.5, 1.52, 0), [1, 1, 1];
}

/// Smoked glass, neutral grey tint over 6 mm. Returns material and colour.
@material smoked() {
  return make(0.05, 1.52, 6), [0.35, 0.35, 0.37];
}

/// Amber glass, the bottle tint, over 5 mm. Returns material and colour.
@material amber() {
  return make(0.05, 1.55, 5), [0.95, 0.60, 0.15];
}

// ===========================================================================
// Plastics and liquids
// ===========================================================================

/// Cast acrylic (PMMA), clearer than glass with a lower index. Returns
/// material and colour.
@material acrylic() {
  return make(0.03, 1.49, 0), [1, 1, 1];
}

/// Polycarbonate, the high-index engineering plastic, with a faint haze.
/// Returns material and colour.
@material polycarbonate() {
  return make(0.08, 1.585, 0), [1, 1, 1];
}

/// Clear SLA resin, a satin finish with a pale amber tint over 8 mm. Returns
/// material and colour.
@material resin() {
  return make(0.15, 1.5, 8), [0.95, 0.85, 0.60];
}

/// Water, with a faint cyan tint over 200 mm. Returns material and colour.
@material water() {
  return make(0, 1.333, 200), [0.80, 0.93, 0.97];
}
